"Benjamin Lewis" <bclewis@cs.sfu.ca> wrote:

> Yes, but they're still trying to get the ball into
> the other team's net, and to keep it out of their
> own.

You just haven't grasped the concept of a wide range
of skills quite yet. I spent several years as a
referee for soccer in a league for kids between ages
five and fourteen [after which they could advance to
junior varsity]. For a five year old kid, the _only_
goal of the game of soccer is "kick the ball". I
call it the "herd of turtles" effect: 22 kids, even
both goalies, in a dense circle around the ball, all
trying to wedge forward to get at least one "toe on
ball" experience. If they managed that, the game was
a success to them. The nets were just distant
abstractions of interest only to adults.

This exactly corresponds to folks like me who fire
up NetHack in order to KILL SOMETHING!

Every once in a while, though, you'd notice a little
kid who'd abandoned the herd of turtles, and was off
admiring the beauty of a mid-field dandilion. For
this kid, too, the day of soccer was entertaining
and a success. That might be Boudewijn, focused on
the inventory and stash management parts of NetHack,
way peripheral to "winning the game", but the part
of the game he seems to enjoy the most.

> It doesn't matter if they ever *get* to the
> goal,

For neither behavior, at this skill level, was it
necessary to worry about "fixing the game of soccer"
so that only net-oriented behaviors were possible.

My job as referee was mostly limited to making sure
some parent didn't die of laughing too hard.

> but if they stop trying the game loses its
> meaning.

Only if you insist on assigning to the game some
meaning other than "having a good time". Type A
personalities tend to suffer that kind of failure
frequently, the rest of us can only shake our heads
in pity and vote you off the entertainment
committee.

I _start_ every NetHack game with the intention to
ascend, but along the way, Things Happen. If I let
ascension as a goal ruin my enjoyment of the sheer
playability of the game, I wouldn't have been
playing it for 17-odd years now, I'd have given up
long ago as ascension proved impossible for my
personality flaw combinations.

> They *could* just sit down in the middle of the
> field and meditate, and they might enjoy this, but
> we shouldn't change the rules of soccer to make
> this easier.

At some skill levels, the soccer players do
_precisely_ that, and the game doesn't require any
changes at all to accomodate them, nor does it
require changes to reject them as players.

At the other end of the skill range I refereed,
you'd better make sure you knew the rulebook by
heart, because the players all did, and if you
messed up something as straightforward as an
off-sides call your reputation as a referee was
toast.

At this player skill level, I had to pay a lot more
attention to the game, but I also had to pay even
more attention to keeping parents under control so
they didn't incite the kids to get hurt. It was only
at this level that I was forced once to red-card a
referee, and tell him either leave the field or his
team would forfeit the game. [He was yelling
encouragement at his players to injure players on
the opposite team, forgetting perhaps that the
effects of sports injuries often linger for life.]

Too much insistence on competition over fun is bad
for any game, and is why professional sports
completely bore me.

Too much focus on assuring that all players follow
identical paths to success is Bad for NetHack, IMO,
and if pursued as a design philosophy, will devalue
the game for the majority of players.

